{
  "duration": 474.7200000000135,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 98.11200000000282
    },
    {
      "start": 98.11200000000282,
      "end": 194.23200000000557
    },
    {
      "start": 194.23200000000557,
      "end": 288.8880000000083
    },
    {
      "start": 288.8880000000083,
      "end": 380.80800000001085
    },
    {
      "start": 380.80800000001085,
      "end": 474.7200000000135
    }
  ],
  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 1.5199999809265137,
      "text": "Hey, welcome back.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 2.3399999141693115,
      "end": 6.529743457451845,
      "text": "Today light is doing something rather surprising: it can knock electrons out",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 6.529743457451845,
      "end": 6.639999866485596,
      "text": "of",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 6.639999866485596,
      "end": 6.980000019073486,
      "text": "a metal.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 7.659999847412109,
      "end": 9.359999656677246,
      "text": "This is the photoelectric effect.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 10.279999732971191,
      "end": 14.191357895180031,
      "text": "Think of an electron as needing an energy payment before it can leave the",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 14.191357895180031,
      "end": 14.619999885559082,
      "text": "surface.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 15.539999961853027,
      "end": 18.979999542236328,
      "text": "Making the light brighter does not necessarily make that payment large enough.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 19.899999618530273,
      "end": 23.360000610351562,
      "text": "Hertz observed that ultraviolet light influenced electrical discharge.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 24.280000686645508,
      "end": 28.15999984741211,
      "text": "Later, Lenard studied the emitted electrons and how their energy responded to",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 28.15999984741211,
      "end": 28.540000915527344,
      "text": "the light.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 29.020000457763672,
      "end": 31.760000228881836,
      "text": "The important observations are these.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 32.099998474121094,
      "end": 36.099998474121094,
      "text": "Below a threshold frequency, ordinary photoelectric emission does not occur,",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 36.63999938964844,
      "end": 39.70000076293945,
      "text": "however bright the light is in the usual single photon model.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 40.560001373291016,
      "end": 44.540000915527344,
      "text": "Above threshold, emission can begin promptly, without a measurable waiting",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 44.540000915527344,
      "end": 47.02000045776367,
      "text": "period for electrons to accumulate energy.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 47.900001525878906,
      "end": 51.2599983215332,
      "text": "Increasing frequency increases the maximum electron energy.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 52.099998474121094,
      "end": 55.81999969482422,
      "text": "Increasing intensity at a fixed suitable frequency mainly supplies more",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 55.81999969482422,
      "end": 59.279998779296875,
      "text": "photons, rather than making each photon more energetic.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 60.34000015258789,
      "end": 60.63999938964844,
      "text": "Pause here.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 61.36000061035156,
      "end": 64.91999816894531,
      "text": "Would a brighter red lamp necessarily release electrons from a surface that",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 64.91999816894531,
      "end": 66.12000274658203,
      "text": "responds to ultraviolet light?",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 66.66000366210938,
      "end": 66.9800033569336,
      "text": "No.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 67.80000305175781,
      "end": 69.9800033569336,
      "text": "Brightness and photon energy are different quantities.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 70.91999816894531,
      "end": 74.86000061035156,
      "text": "Einstein explained these observations by treating light's energy transfer as",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 74.86000061035156,
      "end": 76.5,
      "text": "discrete packets called photons.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 77.16000366210938,
      "end": 80.33999633789062,
      "text": "Each photon carries energy h times frequency.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 80.9000015258789,
      "end": 85.04000091552734,
      "text": "This particle like interaction does not erase light's wave behavior in",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 85.04000091552734,
      "end": 86.23999786376953,
      "text": "interference or diffraction.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 87.19999694824219,
      "end": 89.19999694824219,
      "text": "Different experiments reveal different aspects.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 90.04000091552734,
      "end": 94.95999908447266,
      "text": "For JEE, keep those three observations separate: threshold, prompt emission,",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 95.45999908447266,
      "end": 97.76000213623047,
      "text": "and the distinct roles of frequency and intensity.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 98.11200000000282,
      "end": 100.71199990463539,
      "text": "Let's turn the energy payment into an equation.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 101.49200011444374,
      "end": 104.21199990463539,
      "text": "A photon transfers energy to an electron.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 104.91200019073769,
      "end": 108.59199954223915,
      "text": "The electron first needs the work function, phi, to escape the surface.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 109.2920003051786,
      "end": 111.69199992370888,
      "text": "Whatever remains can appear as kinetic energy.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 112.45200015259071,
      "end": 117.41199923706337,
      "text": "For the most energetic emitted electrons, K maximum equals h f minus phi.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 118.45200015259071,
      "end": 122.07199908447548,
      "text": "Other emitted electrons can have less energy because of their initial states",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 122.07199908447548,
      "end": 124.09199954223915,
      "text": "and interactions inside the material.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 125.0420003051786,
      "end": 129.27199984741492,
      "text": "The work function is a property of the particular surface, not a universal",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 129.27199984741492,
      "end": 130.5519986267118,
      "text": "constant for all metals.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 131.59199954223914,
      "end": 135.6920018310575,
      "text": "When wavelength is given, photon energy is h c divided by wavelength.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 135.6920018310575,
      "end": 140.57199908447546,
      "text": "Here is a practical shortcut: h c is approximately twelve hundred forty",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 140.57199908447546,
      "end": 142.371998321536,
      "text": "electron volts times nanometers.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 143.49200106811804,
      "end": 147.51200152588171,
      "text": "Suppose phi is two electron volts and the incident wavelength is four hundred",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 147.51200152588171,
      "end": 148.0519986267118,
      "text": "nanometers.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 149.371998321536,
      "end": 153.23199893188757,
      "text": "Photon energy is twelve hundred forty divided by four hundred, or three point",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 153.23199893188757,
      "end": 154.3520016784696,
      "text": "one electron volts.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 155.53199816894812,
      "end": 156.6120000000028,
      "text": "Subtract the escape energy.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 157.3520016784696,
      "end": 160.65200091553015,
      "text": "The maximum kinetic energy is one point one electron volts.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 161.53199816894812,
      "end": 165.37200213623328,
      "text": "Your turn: could seven hundred nanometer light cause emission from this same",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 165.37200213623328,
      "end": 165.57199908447546,
      "text": "surface?",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 166.25199938965125,
      "end": 166.75199938965125,
      "text": "Take a moment.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 167.35199786377234,
      "end": 171.41200305176062,
      "text": "Its photon energy is about one point seven seven electron volts, below the",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 171.41200305176062,
      "end": 172.07199908447546,
      "text": "required two.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 172.35199786377234,
      "end": 173.33200122070593,
      "text": "So there is no emission.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 174.1120000000028,
      "end": 175.83200122070593,
      "text": "Do not report a negative kinetic energy.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 176.41200305176062,
      "end": 179.311996948245,
      "text": "At threshold the maximum kinetic energy reaches zero.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 179.87200213623328,
      "end": 184.13866727702106,
      "text": "The longest wavelength that can just satisfy the energy requirement is twelve",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 184.13866727702106,
      "end": 187.49199725342078,
      "text": "hundred forty divided by two, or six hundred twenty nanometers.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 188.45199633789343,
      "end": 190.63199664306921,
      "text": "Shorter wavelengths have higher photon energies.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 191.37200213623328,
      "end": 193.83200122070593,
      "text": "Longer wavelengths fail the threshold condition in this model.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 194.23200000000557,
      "end": 197.9320000476893,
      "text": "Now imagine two lamps of the same color shining on the same surface.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 198.4920002288874,
      "end": 198.9920002288874,
      "text": "One is brighter.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 199.71200001907906,
      "end": 203.1120001144465,
      "text": "Because their frequencies match, their photons carry the same individual",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 203.1120001144465,
      "end": 203.47199977112373,
      "text": "energy.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 204.17199958038887,
      "end": 206.59199965668282,
      "text": "The brighter lamp supplies more photons each second.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 207.29200041962227,
      "end": 211.0519996948298,
      "text": "If emission is possible and the collection conditions are suitable, more",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 211.0519996948298,
      "end": 214.61199916077217,
      "text": "electrons can be emitted and collected, giving a larger photocurrent.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 215.31199992371162,
      "end": 219.23200000000557,
      "text": "But the maximum energy of an emitted electron does not increase merely because",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 219.23200000000557,
      "end": 220.8320003814753,
      "text": "more identical photons arrive.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 220.8320003814753,
      "end": 223.25200045776924,
      "text": "Here is a tiny numerical example.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 223.89199984741768,
      "end": 227.8602065464956,
      "text": "At a fixed frequency, doubling incident power doubles the incident photon",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 227.8602065464956,
      "end": 228.13200152588448,
      "text": "rate.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 228.75200045776924,
      "end": 232.65199816895088,
      "text": "If quantum efficiency and collection remain unchanged, the collected current",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 232.65199816895088,
      "end": 233.59200061035713,
      "text": "doubles too.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 234.2720009155329,
      "end": 238.0519996948298,
      "text": "That proportionality needs those assumptions; it is not a promise for every",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 238.0519996948298,
      "end": 240.09200061035713,
      "text": "real surface at every operating condition.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 240.83199847412666,
      "end": 242.39199984741768,
      "text": "Now change the color instead.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 243.15199816895088,
      "end": 245.09200061035713,
      "text": "Increasing frequency raises the energy per photon.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 245.09200061035713,
      "end": 248.53199923706612,
      "text": "With the same work function, that raises K maximum.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 249.31200183106026,
      "end": 253.57200015259346,
      "text": "However, if total power is fixed, increasing photon energy reduces the number",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 253.57200015259346,
      "end": 254.99199832153877,
      "text": "of photons arriving per second.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 255.75200045776924,
      "end": 258.15199816895085,
      "text": "Real emission efficiency can also change with frequency.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 258.992002136236,
      "end": 262.43199694824773,
      "text": "Therefore, never automatically say that photocurrent must increase with",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 262.43199694824773,
      "end": 262.73200000000554,
      "text": "frequency.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 263.5119987793024,
      "end": 264.6919990844782,
      "text": "Separate energy from rate.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 265.43199694824773,
      "end": 269.23302312137474,
      "text": "Think for a second: same frequency, three times the intensity, above",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 269.23302312137474,
      "end": 269.7919975585993,
      "text": "threshold.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 270.43199694824773,
      "end": 271.7720009155329,
      "text": "What happens to the stopping potential?",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 272.25199664307195,
      "end": 276.5384086131616,
      "text": "It stays the same in the ideal model, although the saturation current can",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 276.5384086131616,
      "end": 276.83199847412664,
      "text": "rise.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 277.45200122070867,
      "end": 281.5920006103571,
      "text": "Below threshold, more ordinary single photon illumination still cannot solve",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 281.5920006103571,
      "end": 282.43199694824773,
      "text": "the energy deficit.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 283.1720024414118,
      "end": 287.15199816895085,
      "text": "This distinction is one of the most useful photoelectric traps to recognize in",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 287.15199816895085,
      "end": 288.0920006103571,
      "text": "a JEE question.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 288.8880000000083,
      "end": 292.7680001144492,
      "text": "How do we measure the maximum electron energy without following individual",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 292.7680001144492,
      "end": 293.2480001335227,
      "text": "electrons?",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 293.8880000000083,
      "end": 295.4479999427878,
      "text": "We use a collector and a voltage.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 296.18800019074314,
      "end": 298.96799992371433,
      "text": "A sufficiently positive collector attracts emitted electrons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 299.8080000763022,
      "end": 303.46799992371433,
      "text": "As collection becomes complete, the current approaches a saturation value.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 304.2079996948325,
      "end": 307.81569241333835,
      "text": "A negative collector instead pushes electrons back toward the emitting",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 307.81569241333835,
      "end": 308.22800015259617,
      "text": "surface.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 309.1879992370688,
      "end": 312.7510767235472,
      "text": "Increase this retarding voltage until even the fastest emitted electrons",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 312.7510767235472,
      "end": 313.0479998474204,
      "text": "cannot",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 313.0479998474204,
      "end": 314.2580008392417,
      "text": "reach the collector.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 314.2580008392417,
      "end": 318.488000381478,
      "text": "That voltage magnitude is the stopping potential, V s.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 318.488000381478,
      "end": 322.98799847412937,
      "text": "The electric energy barrier is electron charge magnitude times V s.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 322.98799847412937,
      "end": 326.72800015259617,
      "text": "So K maximum equals e V s.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 326.72800015259617,
      "end": 330.8880000000083,
      "text": "Notice our convention: V s is positive as a magnitude, even though the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 330.8880000000083,
      "end": 333.34799908448093,
      "text": "collector voltage relative to the emitter is negative at stopping.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 334.06800030518406,
      "end": 337.96800183106296,
      "text": "In a current versus collector voltage graph, the stopping intercept therefore",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 337.96800183106296,
      "end": 339.5279993896567,
      "text": "lies on the negative voltage side.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 340.16799877930515,
      "end": 343.6879992370688,
      "text": "Return to the four hundred nanometer example with a two electron volt work",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 343.6879992370688,
      "end": 343.9480013732993,
      "text": "function.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 344.74800061035984,
      "end": 347.5279993896567,
      "text": "The maximum kinetic energy was one point one electron volts.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 348.5479998474204,
      "end": 350.7079996948325,
      "text": "The stopping magnitude is one point one volts.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 351.6080012207114,
      "end": 355.0279993896567,
      "text": "No conversion to joules is required if we use the electron volt definition",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 355.0279993896567,
      "end": 355.6080012207114,
      "text": "consistently.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 356.4079966430747,
      "end": 360.26799725342624,
      "text": "If we do use joules, divide the energy by the elementary charge to obtain",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 360.26799725342624,
      "end": 360.6279978637778,
      "text": "volts.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 361.46800183106296,
      "end": 364.2279963378989,
      "text": "Now picture two intensity curves at the same frequency.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 364.947997558602,
      "end": 368.84799908448093,
      "text": "The brighter light has the higher saturation current, but both curves meet the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 368.84799908448093,
      "end": 370.56800030518406,
      "text": "voltage axis at the same stopping intercept.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 371.5279993896567,
      "end": 375.2079996948325,
      "text": "At a higher frequency, that intercept shifts farther into negative collector",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 375.2079996948325,
      "end": 375.6080012207114,
      "text": "voltage.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 376.36800335694187,
      "end": 380.24800061035984,
      "text": "Energy changes move the stopping position; photon rate changes mainly change",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 380.24800061035984,
      "end": 380.6279978637778,
      "text": "the height.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 380.80800000001085,
      "end": 384.5280000286211,
      "text": "Let's finish with the graph that turns the whole chapter into one line.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 385.6280001716722,
      "end": 389.18800011445177,
      "text": "Plot maximum kinetic energy vertically and frequency horizontally.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 390.12799969483507,
      "end": 394.007999809276,
      "text": "Above threshold, Einstein's equation gives a straight line with slope h.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 394.9080003814806,
      "end": 398.3280004577745,
      "text": "Its frequency axis intercept is phi divided by h.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 399.0680002288927,
      "end": 403.0080007629503,
      "text": "Extending the algebraic line backward gives a negative vertical intercept, but",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 403.0080007629503,
      "end": 405.92800083924425,
      "text": "that extension is not a physical branch of emitted electrons.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 406.5680002288927,
      "end": 410.6079992370714,
      "text": "Below threshold there are no emitted electrons to assign that negative kinetic",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 410.6079992370714,
      "end": 411.3480009155382,
      "text": "energy to.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 412.0680002288927,
      "end": 415.5879987793077,
      "text": "For the stopping potential graph, the slope is h divided by e.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 416.38800183106554,
      "end": 418.56799832154405,
      "text": "Here is an original JEE style example.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 419.5879987793077,
      "end": 423.21364062657585,
      "text": "Use h approximately four point one four times ten to the minus fifteen",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 423.21364062657585,
      "end": 423.62799969483507,
      "text": "electron",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 423.62799969483507,
      "end": 424.20800152588976,
      "text": "volt seconds.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 425.0080007629503,
      "end": 427.22799816895616,
      "text": "A surface has work function two electron volts.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 428.0879987793077,
      "end": 432.3479999618639,
      "text": "At frequency six times ten to the fourteen hertz, photon energy is two point",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 432.3479999618639,
      "end": 433.54800167847765,
      "text": "four eight four electron volts.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 434.48800030518663,
      "end": 435.12799969483507,
      "text": "Subtract two.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 435.12799969483507,
      "end": 439.5080007629503,
      "text": "K maximum is zero point four eight four electron volts, and the stopping",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 439.5080007629503,
      "end": 441.88800183106554,
      "text": "magnitude is zero point four eight four volts.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 442.86800137330187,
      "end": 446.6079992370714,
      "text": "At eight times ten to the fourteen hertz, photon energy is three point three",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 446.6079992370714,
      "end": 447.6479963379015,
      "text": "one two electron volts.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 448.70800152588976,
      "end": 451.38800183106554,
      "text": "The stopping magnitude becomes one point three one two volts.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 452.3679975586046,
      "end": 455.528001220714,
      "text": "Before calculating anything new, predict the direction of the change.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 456.62799969483507,
      "end": 460.0879987793077,
      "text": "Higher frequency means a larger surplus after paying the same escape cost.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 461.22799816895616,
      "end": 461.50799694825304,
      "text": "Good.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 461.50799694825304,
      "end": 465.70800152588976,
      "text": "When two surfaces have different work functions, their energy frequency lines",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 465.70800152588976,
      "end": 468.30800000001085,
      "text": "have the same slope but different thresholds.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 469.40799847413194,
      "end": 473.6479963379015,
      "text": "Always identify which variable changed, check emission first, and only then",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 473.6479963379015,
      "end": 474.40799847413194,
      "text": "apply the equation.",
      "chapter": 4
    }
  ]
}
